package agentsdoc

import "strings"

// writeV04bDefaultsSection documents the defaults of the current language (a
// `<tui>` without `version`) in a section of their own, one item per change
// of SPEC §31.7, each with the behavior a pinned document keeps: content-sized
// col/row, the sibling-fr rule for viewports only, the fr freeze, content-sized
// modals and hints, the esc close, the modal trap and its idiom, tabs in the
// focus cycle, the built-in focus look, and an input's width (SPEC §22,
// v0.4b). It names no path of the repository, since the project briefing
// (ProjectMarkdown) is this same text.
func writeV04bDefaultsSection(b *strings.Builder) {
	b.WriteString("### v0.4b: the defaults of the current language\n\n")
	b.WriteString("A `<tui>` without `version` lays out and handles keys with the defaults below. A document that writes `version=\"1\"`, `\"2\"`, or `\"3\"` keeps the 0.4.1 behavior each item names, and writing `version=\"3\"` on a document keeps all of it; to see what a document owes to these defaults, run `dump --format json` at 80 and 120 columns on it and on a copy that writes `version=\"3\"`, and diff the two. `tuimark inspect` shows the built-in rules as `ua`. Each item names its fix.\n\n")
	b.WriteString("- **Content-sized `col` and `row`.** An unsized `col` or `row` is `1fr` × `1fr` only as a direct child of a `screen`, a `modal`, or a `tab` (the built-in sheet's `screen > col, screen > row, modal > col, modal > row, tab > col, tab > row { width: 1fr; height: 1fr; }`; a modal's parent is its screen, and the `col`/`row` children an `each` container generates are children of that container). Anywhere else it is `auto` on its parent's main axis and fills the cross axis, so a sidebar `col` beside a body `col` is as wide as its content and the body needs `width: 1fr` (or `flex: 1`), and a header or status `row` written directly in a `screen` is still `1fr` and needs `height: 1`. There is no `flex-shrink`: an `auto` pane never shrinks, panes wider than their row push the last ones out (`L009`, whose message names the fix), and a `wrap: wrap` text in an `auto` pane is measured unwrapped and does not wrap. A `%` child of such a `col` or `row` is `L002`; `justify` takes effect where `1fr` children used to fill the container, and a `spacer` in a container that is now `auto` on its axis has no leftover to take. A pinned document gives every `col` and `row` `1fr` and splits such a row between its `col`s in equal parts.\n")
	b.WriteString("- **The sibling-fr rule, for viewports only.** An unsized sibling of an `fr` child stays `auto` and shares only what the `fr` children leave, except a viewport (`scroll`, `list`, `table`, or an `overflow: scroll` container), which becomes `1fr` on its parent's main axis. A sibling is `fr` when its main size is an `fr` size or a `flex` weight above 0, written or from the sheet (a `spacer`, and a `col`/`row` child of `screen`, `modal`, or `tab`); a viewport made `1fr` by the rule does not count, so two unsized lists side by side stay `auto`, and the split does not depend on the data (a list shares with an `fr` pane however many rows it has). So a `box`, `tabs`, or `hints` next to an `fr` pane takes its content size, a `list`, `scroll`, or `table` still shares with it, and a filter `row` above a list in a `screen`, `modal`, or `tab` is `1fr` and splits the space with the list: give the row `height: 1`. A `text` or a `button` is `auto` in every language. A pinned document also makes an unsized `box` next to an `fr` child `1fr`.\n")
	b.WriteString("- **The `fr` freeze.** `fr` children share what the other children leave as CSS shares flexible lengths: a child whose share its `min-*` raises or its `max-*` lowers is frozen at that size, and the others share what is left again, round after round. Three `1fr` children in 40 columns with `max-width: 10` on the first are 10, 15, and 15, and with `min-width: 30` on the first they are 30, 5, and 5. A `min-*` larger than what is left keeps its size and overflows (`L003`). A pinned document splits first and clamps after: 10, 13, and 14 with 3 columns left empty, and 30, 13, and 14 with an `L009`.\n")
	b.WriteString("- **Content-sized modals and hints.** A `modal` with no `width` (or `height`) is as big as its content on that axis, title, border, and padding included, capped by its `max-width` (`max-height`) and otherwise by 80% of the screen; a pinned document gives it 80% × 80%. Content over the cap is clipped at the modal's content box and reported as `L009` unless `overflow: hidden` is written on the modal, a `%` child on an axis where the modal is content-sized is `L002`, and a `col`/`row` child of the modal is `1fr`, so it takes what the other children leave (its content size when everything fits). The modal does not grow or move while the user types. A `hints` is as big as its items along their axis (`layout: row; gap: 2` in the sheet) and shrinks: the items that do not fit are dropped, with no diagnostic, and a container no longer reports `L009` for a `hints` it cuts. A pinned `hints` is an unsized `box` and shares its row with an `fr` sibling (20 of 40 columns next to a `spacer`).\n")
	b.WriteString("- **`esc` closes a modal.** The top open modal that has no `on:escape` closes on `esc` by writing `false` at its `open` path (its `bind` path when it has no `open`), over whatever the path held; the next frame fires `on:close` and gives focus back, and no event fires for the key itself. A modal whose `open` is `true`, `false`, or `!path`, or whose path a `Derive` computes, stays open, because the runtime has nothing to write, and one with `on:escape` leaves the choice to that action: keep a modal open on purpose (a game-over screen) with one of these. A pinned document does nothing on `esc` without `on:escape`.\n")
	b.WriteString("- **The modal trap and `<keymap when=\"modal\">`.** While a modal is open, a keymap row fires only when it has an effective `when` that matches the chain from the focused node up to the top modal (`when=\"#dlg\"` inside the modal `dlg`, `when=\"modal\"` inside any modal); a row with no `when`, or whose `when` names something outside the modal, is not tried, so `q` cannot quit the app, and the arrows cannot move the board behind it, while a confirmation is open. `tab`, `shift+tab`, `ctrl+c`, and the keys the focused widget takes for itself still work (a `ctrl+c` row without `when` gives way to the built-in `quit`), and `<hints/>` shows only the rows that would fire, often none. A key that should also work under a modal is written again in `<keymap when=\"modal\">`, a group whose rows all carry that `when` (a row's own `when` replaces it). A pinned document's rows fire under a modal.\n")
	b.WriteString("- **`tabs` in the focus cycle, focus kept on the strip.** A `tabs` is focusable without `focusable=\"true\"` (`focusable=\"false\"` turns it off): `tab` and `shift+tab` reach it, `left` and `right` switch the tab, and focus on the strip stays on the strip when the active tab changes, where in a pinned document it moves into the new tab. A form's first `tab` may therefore land on the strip, a script that counts `tab` presses changes, and a `screen focus=` keeps the initial focus where the document wants it. A pinned `tabs` takes focus only with `focusable=\"true\"`.\n")
	b.WriteString("- **The built-in focus look.** The sheet ends with `button:focus, checkbox:focus, select:focus { reverse: true; }` (specificity 11), so a focused `button`, `checkbox`, or `select` is reversed over its whole rect with no author rule. An `input` gets none, since the terminal cursor shows its focus, and a focused `list`, `table`, or `tabs` keeps its own look (the cursor row, the active tab). An open option list is not reversed with its select (`select > list` has `reverse: false`). An author rule wins by origin: `button:focus { reverse: true; }` changes nothing, and another focus style adds `reverse: false` (`.k:focus { reverse: false; bold: true; }`). A pinned document has no such rule: it writes its own.\n")
	b.WriteString("- **An `input`'s width.** An `input` with no `width` is `max(width(placeholder) + 1, 20)` columns whatever its value (a `secret` input's too), with the resolved placeholder, so typing never moves it, the nodes after it, or a modal sized by its content around it; write `width` (`1fr` fills the row) for another size. A pinned `input` is `max(width(value), width(placeholder)) + 1` wide and follows its value.\n")
	b.WriteString("- **The rest of the sheet, and what does not change.** The sheet also gives `table` `wrap: truncate`, `checkbox` and `select` `width: auto; height: auto; wrap: truncate`, and `select > list` `border: single; max-height: 12`. The cross axis (an unsized `col`, `row`, or `box` fills it), `layout: grid` cells, the children of a viewport along its scroll axes, docked children, tables, and every pinned document do not change. In a keymap's `keys`, `ctrl+i`, `ctrl+j`, and `ctrl+m` are `V003` in the current language, since a terminal delivers them as `tab` and `enter`.\n\n")
}
